{{also|Fickle}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪk.əl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-fickle.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪkəl|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|fikil}}, {{m|enm|fikel}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ficol||fickle, cunning, tricky, deceitful}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|fike|le}}. More at {{m|en|fike}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# Quick to change one’s [[opinion]] or [[allegiance]]; [[insincere]]; not [[loyal]] or [[reliable]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|act=III|scene=v|page=69|passage=O Fortune, Fortune, all men call thee '''fickle''', / If thou art '''fickle''', what doſt thou with him / That is renown'd for faith? be '''fickle''' Fortune: / For then I hope thou wilt not keepe him long, / But ſend him backe.}}
#* {{quote-song
|en
|author=James Murphy
|authorlink=James Murphy (electronic musician)
|title=Home
|album=This Is Happening
|artist=LCD Soundsystem
|year=2010
|passage=As night has such a local ring / And love and rock are '''fickle''' things}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[changeable|Changeable]].
#* '''2014,''' {{w|Paul Salopek}}, ''Blessed. Cursed. Claimed.'', National Geographic (December 2014)[https://web.archive.org/web/20150212214621/http://ngm.nationalgeographic.com/2014/12/pilgrim-roads/salopek-text]
#*: {{quote|en|To the south, the vast geometrical deserts of Arabian nomads, a redoubt of feral movement, of '''fickle''' winds, of open space, of saddle leather—home to the wild Bedouin tribes.}}

